{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789789537,"nonce":"prHnTg9RQmRnPcfifqNE89dRJWU=","title":"Python: a for loop's checked item read ran before the loop","message":"`for_sequence` computed the loop item with `index_value`, which hoists the\nchecked read of a dynamic element (a dict's keys read as their kind,\n`zb_any_as_str`) into `self.hoisted`, then bound the target into the body\nwith `bind`. The hoist was drained by the statement protocol ahead of the\nwhole loop statement, so the check and the read ran once before the loop\nand the body bound the previous iteration's value: `for k in d: print(k)`\nprinted `null`, then the first key.\n\nVisible once dict keys were typed (before, the item was Object and no read\nwas hoisted). Fix: the body starts with what the item read hoisted, and\nthe binding goes through `bind_after`.","files":null}]}